00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.280
It’s probably hard to ignore the very obvious&nbsp;
green algae taking up most of your screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.760
But for the moment, I want you to try. Look away&nbsp;
from the closterium and instead, shift your focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.600
to the area that surrounds it. At first, it may&nbsp;
not seem that interesting. It’s just a sea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.000
dark blue, interrupted only by tiny little dots.
But unlike the completely static closterium, those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.880
tiny little dots are wiggling around in&nbsp;
frantic, uncoordinated directions like they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.720
dancing to a million different soundtracks&nbsp;
played at once. There are organisms in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:15.120
world of the microcosmos that move in concerted,&nbsp;
directed ways, but this is definitely not that.&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.720
Now let’s focus on the closterium. Look towards&nbsp;
the tip of the algae, where there are round barium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.840
crystals wiggling around in the same random&nbsp;
way that those particles we saw before were.&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:35.280
We’re going to see that type of motion over&nbsp;
and over again today because it’s all over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.760
microcosmos, found in and around many different&nbsp;
types of organisms. And this kind of random motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.880
may seem almost too trivial to discuss, leading&nbsp;
at best to an end where I mull over the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:55.040
randomness seems to pop up everywhere like some&nbsp;
kind of unifying force that shapes our lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.600
through millions of tiny coincidences.
Except, well, there’s no point waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.280
until the end to say all that because the&nbsp;
reason we want to talk about this weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:10.080
wiggling motion is that this randomness really&nbsp;
does tie us together, not metaphorically, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:18.000
very, very literally and physically. This motion&nbsp;
that you see is a proof of something fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:26.080
not just to life, but to existence&nbsp;
itself. This movement is proof of atoms.&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.760
It’s easy to take the existence of atoms for&nbsp;
granted now, in the same way that it’s easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.520
take the existence of microbes for granted. Once&nbsp;
you know about them and how they shape the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.160
and you've been told about them since you
were a child,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.760
it’s impossible to see the&nbsp;
world as existing without them.&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.800
But our knowledge of both is relatively recent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.600
even if it’s built on work that spans&nbsp;
millennia. And just as the microscope has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.120
essential to our understanding of microbes, it has&nbsp;
played an important role in understanding atoms.&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.760
Long before the existence of the&nbsp;
microscope though, there was an idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.960
In the fifth century BCE, a Greek philosopher&nbsp;
named Democritus proposed that if you kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.200
breaking matter down further and further, you&nbsp;
would eventually reach something that could no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:24.880
longer be broken down. That indivisible thing was&nbsp;
what he called atomos, which meant “uncuttable”.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.600
Democritus’ theory was in opposition&nbsp;
to the ideas of Aristotle and Plato,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.640
who believed that the world was composed of&nbsp;
four basic elements: earth, wind, air, and fire.&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.920
So what does Democritus have&nbsp;
to do with the tiny little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.920
wobbles of the pigments inside this stentor?
For that, we have to flash forward to 1827.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:49.040
This was an exciting time for anyone interested in&nbsp;
diving deep into what matter was made of. The end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.320
of the 18th century had seen important advances&nbsp;
in our understanding of matter. And in 1803,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.000
the scientist John Dalton drew upon these&nbsp;
ideas to propose that each chemical element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
could be described by a particular atom.
The atom became a useful tool in developing new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.640
theories about how our world is built, and 19th&nbsp;
century physicists used it to describe a theory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.960
gasses that assumed they were composed of many,&nbsp;
many tiny particles constantly moving around.&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.240
But these theories weren’t proof. If anything,&nbsp;
they raised more questions about how to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.720
about atoms. Should we think of atoms as&nbsp;
just some kind of mathematical metaphor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.280
or were they something real? And how could we&nbsp;
find proof of something as unseeable as an atom?&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.840
The answer, it would turn out, would come&nbsp;
thanks to a botanist named Robert Brown.&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.880
Brown wasn’t setting out to solve the&nbsp;
problem of atoms. It was the summer of 1827,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.200
and he was trying to clear up some questions&nbsp;
he had about the tiny particles that burst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.080
out of pollen grains like this one.
So he did what we’re doing right now:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.040
he brought out his microscope, and looked at&nbsp;
some tiny things under it. While we didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.160
those pollen particles on hand, we imagine that&nbsp;
what he saw was similar to these oil droplets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.560
which came from the body of a dying copepod.
He said simply, “While examining the form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.400
these particles immersed in water, I observed&nbsp;
many of them very evidently in motion,”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.160
Curious about this motion, Brown proceeded to do&nbsp;
a number of different experiments. He looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.800
pollen from other plants and saw the same motion.&nbsp;
He looked at pollen from dead plants and saw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.880
same motion. He even moved on to inorganic&nbsp;
materials like rocks and saw the same motion.&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.680
In water, everything wiggled, even if it wasn’t&nbsp;
living. And that meant the motion wasn’t something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.680
biological, it was rooted in something else.
These random movements are what we now call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.600
Brownian motion. And in the decades that followed&nbsp;
his observation, physicists began to study it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.000
in earnest so they could understand&nbsp;
the forces that shaped the movement.&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.360
For example, one scientist noted that smaller&nbsp;
particles exhibited faster motion compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.160
larger ones, which you can see at play here&nbsp;
in the crystals lying within this amoeba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.320
with the larger crystals moving more&nbsp;
slowly compared to the smaller ones5.&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.760
But Brownian motion’s biggest impact would&nbsp;
come in 1905 when a scientist theorized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.640
that the movement of the particles Brown&nbsp;
had watched was the result of other smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:31.667
particles colliding into them. That scientist, you 
probably know the name of, it was Albert Einstein.

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.592
And those smaller&nbsp;particles were 
water molecules, composed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.592 --> 00:06:41.280
atoms that are packed with energy. And because of&nbsp;
that energy, the water molecules are constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.840
moving and colliding. Sometimes they collide with&nbsp;
each other. And sometimes they collide into other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.280
larger particles, which move erratically in&nbsp;
response. That is Brownian motion, a movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.040
shaped by atoms and their kinetic energy.
Einstein’s theories were built on equations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.760
but they provided a framework that would allow&nbsp;
experimentalists to use Brownian motion as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.040
way to see atoms and their energy at work. And&nbsp;
ultimately, it was the French scientist Jean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.240
Perrin who put these theories to the test, using&nbsp;
a new microscope called the ultramicroscope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.000
not only study Brownian motion and confirm the&nbsp;
equations that drove Einstein’s theory, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.858
to estimate the size of water molecules.

00:07:25.858 --> 00:07:29.040
All of that discovery was made possible because a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.760
botanist wanted to study some pollen.&nbsp;
Robert Brown was operating in a realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.440
that is familiar to us here on Journey to the&nbsp;
Microcosmos. He was exploring an invisible world.&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:46.640
But what he studied and what he described helped&nbsp;
us find an even more invisible world, one that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.280
still invisible under his microscope, except&nbsp;
that it was responsible for everything he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:57.680
seeing, not just the motions of the pollen grains,&nbsp;
but the pollen grains themselves and anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.560
that was under his microscope, outside his&nbsp;
microscope, and even the microscope itself.&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.720
What he found in those wiggles—what you’re seeing&nbsp;
now in the same motion more than a century later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.920
is an invisible world that&nbsp;
built an entire universe.&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.240
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

